{"name":"Beryl","hex":"#71dcb8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Beryl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from English derived from the gem beryl.","A male given name from Yiddish, meaning “little bear”."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Beryl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beryl","blocks":[{"text":"Beryl ( BERR-əl) is a mineral composed of beryllium aluminium silicate with the chemical formula Be3Al2(SiO3)6. Well-known varieties of beryl include emerald and aquamarine. Naturally occurring hexagonal crystals of beryl can be up to several meters in size, but terminated crystals are relatively rare. Pure beryl is colorless, but it is frequently tinted by impurities; possible colors are green, blue, yellow, pink, and red (the rarest). It is an ore source of beryllium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Beryl09.jpg/330px-Beryl09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beryl","wikidata":"Q103480"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/500px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/1280px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/B%C3%A9ryl.jpg/500px-B%C3%A9ryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Main beryl producing countries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AB%C3%A9ryl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png/500px-Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Beryl_Crystal_Structure.png/